// Practice quiz: Issuing and checking a JWT
// Minimum release, November 2026: author-checked, no independent review. Formative, low-stakes retention practice. Titles name the objective id each item practises.

$CATEGORY: spring-boot/api-security/issuing-and-checking-a-jwt

::o1 claims::Which claim tells a resource server when a token stops being valid? {
=exp#Correct. The expiry claim.
~sub#Not quite. That is the subject.
~iat#Not quite. That is when it was issued.
~aud#Not quite. That is the audience.
}

::o1 not encrypted::Who can read the payload of a standard signed JWT? {
=Anyone holding the token#Correct. Signing does not hide the content.
~Only the signing server#Not quite. Anyone can decode it.
~Nobody without the key#Not quite. The key is needed to verify or sign, not to read.
~Only the browser#Not quite. Any holder can read it.
}

::o2 validation::What does the resource server check on each request by default? {
=The signature and the time claims such as expiry#Correct. A failed check means rejection.
~That the user is in the database#Not quite. That is not part of default JWT validation.
~The user password#Not quite. A token replaces sending it.
~The client IP address#Not quite. It is not part of this check.
}

::o2 key source::Where should the JWT signing key come from? {
=The environment or a secret store#Correct. It must never be committed.
~Hardcoded in the class#Not quite. That commits it.
~The token itself#Not quite. That would prove nothing.
~A public web page#Not quite. A signing key must stay private.
}

::o3 predictions::A request has an expired token. What status results? {
=401#Correct. The caller is not validly authenticated.
~403#Not quite. That is for an authenticated caller without permission.
~200#Not quite. Expiry is enforced.
~404#Not quite. The path exists.
}

